; ============================================================================
; WORKSPACE IDENTITY LADDER -- AIF-078 D10.1 / D10.2 / D10.3
; ============================================================================
;
; WHAT MAKES THIS A DISCRIMINATOR AND NOT A DEMONSTRATION.
;
; WSL_T4 is the arm that matters. It destroys a workspace and then creates one
; with the SAME NAME, and asserts the second WS_ID is GREATER than the first.
; That comparison can only pass if retirement actually reached the catalog:
; if WORKSPACE DESTROY silently no-ops, or supersedes the wrong row, or writes
; a flag that does not stick, then the name still has a live chain, the second
; NEW ADOPTS it, and the two ids are EQUAL. One line, and it separates a real
; retirement from a cheerful message.
;
; WHY IT ASSERTS AGAINST THE CATALOG TABLE.
;
; WORKSPACES.dbf is an ordinary x64 table -- that is the whole point of it, the
; map drawn in the same ink as the territory -- so this spec opens it and reads
; FIELDS. Every marker here is a field-value comparison, per the doctrine
; FIELDMGR_APPEND paid for: a spec that asserts SHAPE passes green on a blanked
; table. Nothing here reads a console line.
;
; WHAT IT DELIBERATELY DOES NOT COVER, stated rather than implied.
;
; ADOPTION ACROSS A PROCESS BOUNDARY is not testable here. WORKSPACE NEW
; refuses a duplicate name within a session, so a single process cannot ask a
; second NEW to adopt. It was proven by hand instead, 2026-08-23, build
; 05:27:07, two separate datarun processes:
;
;     run 1: WORKSPACE NEW: handle 2  name LADDERTEST ... WS_ID 110
;     run 2: WORKSPACE NEW: handle 2  name LADDERTEST ... WS_ID 110
;            ADOPTED the durable identity this name already holds ...
;
; A two-process fixture is the chartered follow-up. Until it exists, adoption
; rests on that transcript and this spec does not claim it.
;
; NOTHING HERE ASSERTS THAT ANYTHING IS ABSENT. USE_AGAIN established over
; three cuts that no marker in this language can assert emptiness, and that an
; ERRORED marker PRINTS NOTHING rather than going red -- so a suite can lose a
; claim silently and still report a full green. Both refusal arms therefore ask
; the answerable question instead: did the KNOWN OCCUPANT SURVIVE.
;
; GUARDS. If any WSL_G* reds, treat every WSL_T* as UNPROVEN rather than as
; passing -- the catalog predicates depend on locating the right row, and the
; two refusal arms depend on a sentinel actually having been written.
;
; WSL_G4/G5 EXIST BECAUSE THIS SPEC'S FIRST RUN NEEDED THEM. It wrote APPEND
; BLANK, which this shell does not take; nothing was appended, both sentinel
; tables were created EMPTY, and WSL_T6 and WSL_T7 both went RED while the verb
; under test had behaved perfectly -- the refusals printed exactly as designed.
; A fixture failure wearing a verb failure's clothes. The guards were missing
; because this spec guarded its CATALOG reads and not its SENTINEL writes, which
; is the same omission WSMULTI's WSM_G4 was added to close. Guard every input an
; arm reads, not just the interesting ones.
;
; Self-bootstrapping: WSLDEF / WSLOCC in SANDBOX, erased at the end. Leaves
; retired (SUPERSEDED) catalog rows behind BY DESIGN -- that is the history
; D10.3 keeps, and it is what WSL_T5 reads.
; ============================================================================

SET PATH DBF DBF/SANDBOX
WORKSPACE CLOSE ALL

; Prior-run residue: these may or may not exist. A refusal here is noise, not
; a failure -- the spec's own arms do not depend on the starting state.
WORKSPACE DESTROY WSLADR1
WORKSPACE DESTROY WSLADR2

ERASE wsldef CONFIRM
ERASE wslocc CONFIRM

; Two sentinels, each in its OWN engine area -- CREATE closes the current area
; before writing, so creating into an area that already holds a sentinel would
; destroy the thing under test. The slot is chosen explicitly, never inherited.
;
; APPEND, not APPEND BLANK. The first cut of this spec wrote APPEND BLANK, which
; this shell does not take: BLANK falls through as an unrecognized argument, the
; command prints its usage, NOTHING IS APPENDED, and the following REPLACE says
; "no current record". Both sentinel tables were created EMPTY and the two
; refusal arms below read a field that was never written -- so a FIXTURE failure
; arrived dressed as a VERB failure. Had the empty field happened to compare
; equal it would have arrived as a GREEN instead, which is the whole reason
; WSL_G4/G5 now exist.

SELECT 0
CREATE X64 WSLDEF (ID N(4), MARK C(10))
APPEND
REPLACE ID WITH 1
REPLACE MARK WITH "DEF"

SELECT 1
CREATE X64 WSLOCC (ID N(4), MARK C(10))
APPEND
REPLACE ID WITH 1
REPLACE MARK WITH "OCC"

; Guards for the two arms that depend on these. A green WSL_T6 or WSL_T7 means
; nothing if the sentinel it reads was never written.
SELECT 0
? "WSL_G4_default_sentinel_written:" + (ALLTRIM(MARK) = "DEF")
SELECT 1
? "WSL_G5_occupant_sentinel_written:" + (ALLTRIM(MARK) = "OCC")

WORKSPACE CLOSE ALL

; --- BIRTH ------------------------------------------------------------------
; D10.1: a workspace is born durable. D10.2: the birth row IS the chain root.

WORKSPACE NEW WSLADR1

&& AIF-078 sec 4: '&_WORKSPACES', NOT 'workspaces'. DO NOT SIMPLIFY THIS BACK.
&& This spec WRITES through the WORKSPACES slot (WORKSPACE NEW) and READS
&& through the DBF slot (USE WORKSPACES). 'SET PATH DBF workspaces' is
&& DATA-relative and therefore PINNED TO PRODUCTION: bracket the WORKSPACES
&& slot for isolation and the mint lands in scratch while the LOCATE below
&& searches production, and every arm goes red for a reason unrelated to what
&& it tests. '&_WORKSPACES' is the engine-owned variable for that slot,
&& resolved from paths::state() at expansion time, so it FOLLOWS the bracket.
&& Proven 2026-08-28: with the slot moved to scratch, 'workspaces' still
&& resolved to data\workspaces while '&_WORKSPACES' resolved to the scratch
&& root. Identical when nothing is bracketed, which is why this is inert today.
SET PATH DBF &_WORKSPACES
USE WORKSPACES
LOCATE FOR ALLTRIM(WS_NAME) = "WSLADR1" .AND. ALLTRIM(SUPERSEDED) = "0"
VAR id1 = WS_ID
? "WSL_G0_live_row_located_by_name:" + (ALLTRIM(WS_NAME) = "WSLADR1")
? "WSL_G1_located_row_is_live:" + (ALLTRIM(SUPERSEDED) = "0")
? "WSL_T1_birth_row_is_self_describing:" + (ALLTRIM(FMT) = "BIRTH 1")
? "WSL_T2_birth_row_carries_no_payload:" + (SIZE_B = 0)
? "WSL_T3_birth_row_is_the_chain_root:" + (PREV_ID = 0)
CLOSE

; --- THE DISCRIMINATOR ------------------------------------------------------
; Destroy, then create the same name again. A fresh id proves the live row was
; really retired; an equal id proves it was not.

WORKSPACE DESTROY WSLADR1
WORKSPACE NEW WSLADR1

SET PATH DBF &_WORKSPACES
USE WORKSPACES
LOCATE FOR ALLTRIM(WS_NAME) = "WSLADR1" .AND. ALLTRIM(SUPERSEDED) = "0"
? "WSL_G2_live_row_located_after_recreate:" + (ALLTRIM(WS_NAME) = "WSLADR1")
? "WSL_T4_destroy_then_new_minted_a_fresh_id:" + (WS_ID > $id1)

; --- HISTORY KEPT -----------------------------------------------------------
; The retired row is still there, still readable, and now flagged. A destroyed
; workspace leaves a record, not a hole.

LOCATE FOR WS_ID = $id1
? "WSL_G3_retired_row_still_present:" + (ALLTRIM(WS_NAME) = "WSLADR1")
? "WSL_T5_retired_row_is_superseded:" + (ALLTRIM(SUPERSEDED) = "1")
CLOSE

; --- REFUSAL: DEFAULT -------------------------------------------------------
; Invariant I1 needs DEFAULT to outlive every other workspace. Asserted by a
; POSITIVE READ of a sentinel opened in DEFAULT afterwards, never by claiming
; something is absent.

SET PATH DBF DBF/SANDBOX
USE wsldef
WORKSPACE DESTROY DEFAULT
? "WSL_T6_default_survived_the_refusal:" + (ALLTRIM(MARK) = "DEF")
WORKSPACE CLOSE ALL

; --- REFUSAL: OCCUPIED ------------------------------------------------------
; Destroy does not cascade, so it must refuse a workspace that still holds an
; area -- and the area must still be there afterwards.

WORKSPACE NEW WSLADR2
WORKSPACE SWITCH WSLADR2
USE wslocc
WORKSPACE DESTROY WSLADR2
? "WSL_T7_occupied_workspace_kept_its_area:" + (ALLTRIM(MARK) = "OCC")

; --- TEARDOWN ---------------------------------------------------------------

WORKSPACE CLOSE ALL
WORKSPACE SWITCH DEFAULT
WORKSPACE DESTROY WSLADR1
WORKSPACE DESTROY WSLADR2
ERASE wsldef CONFIRM
ERASE wslocc CONFIRM
